    id: 60,
    slug: 'st-charles-mayoral-race-challenger-files-2026',
    section: 'local-politics',
    title: 'Former Alderman Files for St. Charles Mayor, Citing Overdevelopment Concerns',
    byline: 'Tribune Staff',
    date: 'May 25, 2026',
    category: 'Elections',
    excerpt: 'Ray Montoya, a former District 4 alderman, filed a nominating petition Friday to challenge Mayor Lori Sheridan in the April 2027 consolidated election.',
    body: [
      'Former St. Charles 4th Ward Alderman Ray Montoya filed a nominating petition Friday afternoon to run for mayor in the April 2027 consolidated election, setting up the first competitive mayoral race in the city since 2019. Montoya, who served two terms on the city council before declining to seek a third in 2021, said the primary motivation for his campaign is opposition to what he calls the current administration\'s permissive stance on high-density residential development.',
      '"In the last three years this city has approved more apartment units than in the previous decade combined," Montoya said outside City Hall after submitting 347 signatures — well above the 200 required for ballot access. "I have nothing against housing growth in principle. But approving seven-story buildings on Second Street without the infrastructure plan to support them is not growth — it is wishful thinking with other people\'s neighborhoods."',
      'Mayor Lori Sheridan, who has held the office since 2015 and won her last race with 61 percent of the vote, declined to comment directly on Montoya\'s entry but said in a statement that she was "proud of St. Charles\'s record of responsible growth that has added tax base, jobs, and amenities while maintaining the character that makes this community exceptional."',
      'Montoya\'s campaign will focus on three issues: requiring infrastructure impact assessments before any residential development above four stories, establishing an independent inspector general\'s office to review city contracts, and freezing the Tax Increment Financing district on Charlestowne Mall until the city\'s TIF audit — initiated by a council resolution in March — is complete.',
      'Filing for the April 2027 ballot runs through November 21, 2026. As of Friday, Sheridan has not filed reelection paperwork, though her office said that decision will be announced "before the end of summer." Political observers note that three of the aldermen elected in the 2023 cycle ran on development-skeptic platforms and that Montoya\'s framing reflects a genuine cleavage in city opinion on growth policy.',
    ],
    tags: ['local-politics', 'elections', 'st-charles', 'government', 'community'],
    locked: true,
    featured: false,
